figdown 0.1 bitfield
# `REPEATED-RUN-DRAWING`: a repeated run is DRAWN as its first element, the
# elision, and its last — RFC 8754 §2's own drawing, which 0.1 already
# took as the authority for a spanning field in the same figure. Before this
# release the engine drew ONE occurrence and hung `[first] … [last]` on the
# elision strip; that strip text is gone, and the index is APPENDED to the
# author's label instead (`Queue Depth [0]`), never inserted into it — the
# label is opaque text and the engine may not parse it to find where the RFC
# would put a bracket.
#
# THE ELISION MARK IS DERIVED FROM ARITHMETIC, not from a special case. The
# elements not drawn number |last − first| − 1, and the mark is drawn when
# that is not zero. The two lines below are the two sides of that:
#   `Determinate run` — index=0..7, 6 undrawn, so the mark IS drawn;
#   `Adjacent pair`   — index=0..1, 0 undrawn, so the mark is NOT drawn.
# The mark means "there are more, not drawn"; over a pair it would be a false
# statement. `index=0..0` never reaches the renderer at all — it is a line
# error (case 421), because a range of one element is not repetition.
#
# WHAT THIS CASE PINS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. The model here is the same model
# 0.1 recorded: `index` is a key on a field, and the number of drawn
# occurrences is a RENDERING decision the model neither carries nor implies —
# exactly the division case 424 states for spanning geometry. So the golden
# pins the index OBJECT (`last` a number = determinate, a string = prose) and
# the field widths and order; it does NOT pin that two boxes are drawn, that
# the elision mark appears over one line and not the other, or any geometry
# whatever. `run.js` has no SVG goldens by design. What the fixture buys is
# that the renderer is EXERCISED over both arms — a change that throws on
# either one fails the run — and that a second implementation reading this
# file is told, in prose, which drawing the reference produces and why.
title "Repetition occurrences"
bitfield rec "Repeated elements (word=32)" word=32 numbering=msb0
field "Head" 16
# Determinate, 8 elements, 6 of them undrawn. Starts mid-row, so the two drawn
# occurrences stand in the same columns one band apart and the cells beside
# them are blank DRAWING, not bits.
field "Determinate run" 16 index=0..7
# Determinate, 2 elements, NONE undrawn: both are drawn and nothing separates
# them but the ordinary boundary between two boxes.
field "Adjacent pair" 32 index=0..1
# Descending: 54 elements, 52 undrawn. The absolute value is what makes this
# a run rather than a negative count.
field "Descending" 32 index=53..0
# Prose last index: the count is not in the document, so the number undrawn is
# indeterminate and the mark is drawn.
field "Prose run" 32 index="0..Entry Count"
# `index=""` claims repetition and states NO index, so there is no [first] and
# no [last] to write on an occurrence — one occurrence and a bare elision
# mark, the same ruling `present=""` gets for its caption.
field "Unstated" 32 index=""
# Wider than `word=` AND repeated — the interaction examples/srh.fd carries.
# Each occurrence spans two rows and is still one field: one box, name once.
field "Spanning run" 64 index=0..3
field "Tail" 16
